course: dictionary
lesson: 461
title: Armful
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An armful is how much you can carry in your arms. «Mole comes back with an armful of rifles.» Mole is carrying as many rifles as his arms can hold, so armful means the amount you can carry.
explain: Look inside the word. Armful has arm in it, and the arms are doing the carrying. This is a helpful clue whenever you meet a word with a smaller word hidden in it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Mole comes back with as much as he can carry in his arms» still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Armful means as much as you can carry in one or both arms.
fact: Armful is a noun, and its plural is armfuls.
fact: You can say an armful of books, an armful of firewood, or by the armful when there are lots.
fact: An armful is a rough amount, not an exact number, so in a maths problem it does not tell you how many things there are.
exercise: «Mole comes back with an armful of rifles.» What does an armful mean? | A single rifle | A small box of tiny things | *As much as you can carry in your arms | A long walk back home
exercise: Which word has the word arm hidden inside it? | Mole | comes | *armful | back
exercise: Ayaan has two loads of firewood, each as much as his arms can hold. What is the plural of armful? | armsful | armfulls | armsfuls | *armfuls
test: Lara says, «I carried an armful of books home.» What does this tell us? | *She carried as many books as she could hold in her arms | She carried one heavy box | She read every book on the shelf | She left the books at school
test: A maths question says, «Ben picks up an armful of apples.» Is the exact number given? | Yes, exactly one apple | Yes, exactly ten apples | *No, it is a rough amount, not an exact number | No, there are no apples at all
exam: Which phrase means in large amounts? | with a loud shout | *by the armful | at the front door | in one hand
